  "text": ".Login to your accountI'm new here, kindly teach me\n\n짢\nLet's calculate the first few terms of the sequence and then find the infinite product.\n\nWe know that \\( x_1 = 2 \\).\n\nFor \\( k \\geq 2 \\), \\( x_k = \\frac{1}{x_{k-1}} + x_{k-1} \\). This means:\n\\[ x_2 = \\frac{1}{x_1} + x_1 = \\frac{1}{2} + 2 = \\frac{5}{2}. \\]\n\\[ x_3 = \\frac{1}{x_2} + x_2 = \\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{5}{2} = \\frac{4 + 25}{10} = \\frac{29}{10}. \\]\n\nWe can calculate a few subsequent terms to confirm the pattern:\n\\[ x_4 = \\frac{1}{x_3} + x_3 = \\frac{10}{29} + \\frac{29}{10} = \\frac{100 + 841}{290} = \\frac{941}{290}. \\]\n\nIt appears that \\( x_k \\) is becoming quite large, indicating that the terms of the sequence are diverging from 2 in the positive direction.\n\nNow, let's find the infinite product:\n\\[ \\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right). \\]\n\nWe can break this down step by step:\n\nFor \\( n = 1 \\):\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{1}{x_1} = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = 1.5. \\]\n\nFor \\( n = 2 \\):\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{1}{x_2} = 1 + \\frac{2}{5} = 1.4. \\]\n\nFor \\( n = 3 \\):\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{1}{x_3} = 1 + \\frac{10}{29} = \\frac{39}{29}. \\]\n\nFor \\( n = 4 \\):\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{1}{x_4} = 1 + \\frac{290}{941} = \\frac{1231}{941}. \\]\n\nAs \\( n \\to \\infty \\), \\( x_n \\to \\infty \\), so \\( \\frac{1}{x_n} \\to 0 \\), and thus \\( 1 + \\frac{1}{x_n} \\to 1 \\). However, since the terms are getting closer and closer to 1 but not exactly 1 (due to the quadratic nature of the sequence), we can evaluate the product of the first few terms to approximate the infinite product.\n\nLet's calculate the product of the first 4 terms:\n\\[ \\prod_{n=1}^{4} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right) = 1.5 \\times 1.4 \\times \\frac{39}{29} \\times \\frac{1231}{941}. \\]\n\nCalculating this product:\n\\[ 1.5 \\approx \\frac{3}{2}, \\]\n\\[ 1.4 \\approx \\frac{7}{5}, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{39}{29} \\approx 1.3448, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1231}{941} \\approx 1.3114. \\]\n\n\\[ \\prod_{n=1}^{4} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x_n}\\right) \\approx \\frac{3}{2} \\times \\frac{7}{5} \\times 1.3448 \\times 1.3114. \\]\n\n\\[ \\approx \\frac{3 \\times 7 \\times 1.3448 \\times 1.3114}{2 \\times 5} \\]\n\\[ \\approx \\frac{30.67}{10} \\]\n\\[ \\approx 3.067. \\]\n\nThis product is much smaller than 1, which suggests that the terms of the sequence are getting closer to 1 but not exactly 1. As \\( n \\to \\infty \\), \\( x_n \\to \\infty \\), and thus \\( 1 + \\frac{1}{x_n} \\to 1 \\). Therefore, the infinite product is 1.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
